Listening to the ice

The first thing that strikes you about the Southern Ocean is not the cold, though that is formidable, nor the waves, though on our third day out of port they rose higher than the ship's bridge. It is the noise. Below the waterline, the sea around Antarctica is anything but silent: it crackles, groans and sings with the sounds of shifting ice and the calls of animals that most of us will never see.

{{41}}

What it had to say, as it turned out, was a great deal, and much of it was baffling. On my first evening, Dr Helen Okafor, the expedition's chief scientist, handed me a pair of headphones and invited me to listen to the previous night's recording. I heard a low, rhythmic thudding, like a distant drum, punctuated by eerie whistles that rose and fell in pitch.

{{42}}

Part of the problem is sheer volume. A single hydrophone left in the water for a month generates more audio than a person could listen to in a year. The team relies on software to sift through it, flagging sounds that match known patterns – the clicks of sperm whales, say, or the characteristic crack of an iceberg calving. But anything unfamiliar risks being overlooked, filed away as background noise.

{{43}}

My own hour of listening each day became, unexpectedly, the highlight of the voyage. Life on board was otherwise a matter of routine: meals at fixed times, safety drills, the endless business of securing anything that might slide across a cabin in heavy seas. But with the headphones on, I was somewhere else entirely.

{{44}}

The scientists, of course, have known otherwise for decades. Yet their research has taken on new urgency in recent years, as the noise of human activity – shipping, seismic surveys, construction – has spread into even the remotest waters. Many marine animals depend on sound to find food, navigate and locate one another across great distances. If their world grows too noisy, they may struggle to do any of these things.

{{45}}

On our last night at sea, Helen played me one final recording. It had been made a few hours earlier, near a vast, flat-topped iceberg drifting northwards and slowly breaking apart. The sound was unlike anything I had heard before – a deep, prolonged moan, almost mournful, that seemed to go on for minutes.

{{46}}

The apple detective

Tom Hartley does not look like a detective. A retired agricultural engineer in his seventies, he dresses in a battered waxed jacket and walks with the unhurried gait of someone who has spent a lifetime outdoors. Yet for the past fifteen years he has been engaged in an investigation of sorts, tracking down apple varieties that were once grown across the region and are now believed to be lost.

{{41}}

The tree eventually turned out to be a variety last recorded in a nurseryman's catalogue in 1891. The discovery gave Hartley a taste for the chase, and he has since identified more than forty varieties that had been written off as extinct. Most were found in similar circumstances: in old gardens, abandoned orchards and, on one memorable occasion, the car park of a supermarket.

{{42}}

Identifying a variety is painstaking work. Unlike many plants, apples do not grow true from seed: plant the pip of a particular variety and the resulting tree will produce fruit that may be quite unlike its parent. Every tree of a named variety is therefore, in effect, a clone, propagated by grafting a cutting onto a rootstock. To establish what an unknown tree is, Hartley must compare its fruit, leaves and blossom against descriptions in historical records.

{{43}}

Yet even DNA has its limits. It can show that a mysterious tree matches a sample held in a national collection, but only if that sample has itself been correctly identified – and collections, Hartley points out, have been known to contain errors. In the end, much depends on the patient accumulation of evidence, and on a certain amount of instinct.

{{44}}

Why does any of this matter? To Hartley, the answer is partly practical. Commercial orchards today are dominated by a handful of varieties, chosen for their yield, appearance and ability to withstand long-distance transport. Older varieties, adapted over centuries to local soils and climates, may carry resistance to diseases or tolerance of conditions that will prove valuable as the climate changes.

{{45}}

Before we part, Hartley hands me a paper bag containing three apples from the tree he examined earlier. They are small and slightly lopsided, with a sharp, almost lemony flavour. If his hunch is right, they belong to a variety that has not been officially recorded for over a century.

{{46}}
